{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfssW1BrUKLN93g4zhzcF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I dismissed R39F1 as not worth its cost. The over-denial is real, but it fails closed, and fixing it risks making the hook fail open. The verdict is committed as `c04ea9c8` in `docs/reviews/wi-834/040-ADJUDICATE-73e78ae.md`, with the `WI: WI-834` trailer.\n\n**What I reproduced:**\n- `function Invoke-Review { codex exec … }` is denied during blackout, and the hook reads `['function', 'codex']`.\n- Real PowerShell defines such a function without running its body, so the finding holds.\n- The cause is the round-1 decision to treat PowerShell braces as statement boundaries. That same reading is what correctly denies blocks that do run, such as `& { claude }`, `ForEach-Object { codex … }` and `if (…) { claude }`.\n\n**Why it isn't worth fixing:**\n- **It fails closed.** It bites only inside an armed window, and only for a function whose body names a model CLI. The session is just told to wait, and defining a model-wrapping helper during the interval meant to pause model work gains almost nothing.\n- **The fix is risky.** Separating function bodies that don't run yet from blocks that do needs brace-depth tracking in the very reader this lane has reopened round after round. A misclassification would turn today's correct denials into silent launches.\n- **It would give up a correct denial.** The define-and-call line `function X { codex … }; X` runs a model and is denied today. Under the fix it would read only `X`.\n\n**One observation, outside this finding and not ruled:** Bash's handling of function definitions is inconsistent.\n- `function f { claude; }` is allowed, which the lane's own test pins.\n- `f() { claude; }` is denied, the same kind of fail-closed over-denial.\n- `function f { claude; }; f` is allowed even though it runs claude. Under the sitting-025 line, a function body run through its name counts as a wrapper and is outside coverage, so this is not a coverage defect.\n\nIf the owner wants function bodies treated consistently, that is a coverage decision for both shells together, not a one-round patch to the reader.\n\nMy probe script is in the session scratchpad. The `.requested` marker is left untracked for the coordinator."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2225,"cache_read_input_tokens":554929,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2225},"output_tokens":697,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8d8d1fbc-6a42-4a02-9ec0-8f8d2b61c24c","uuid":"8d8027e7-4321-41bd-905a-222405ada09e","timestamp":"2026-10-10T03:47:29.277Z","request_id":"req_011CfssVzt1WDqmXJnUzA58b"}
